- How would you handle deletion of words in the Trie without breaking suggestions?
- How would you adapt suggest to support prefix similarity (typo-tolerant search)?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem demonstrates how to model a constrained, single-use resource within a shortest-path framework. It tests understanding of state-space expansion and whether a candidate can adapt classic algorithms to modified constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **State-Space Expansion:** Augment each vertex with a binary flag indicating coupon usage
- **Layer 0 (coupon unused):** Can transition to layer 0 (full cost) or layer 1 (half cost)
- **Layer 1 (coupon used):** Can only transition to layer 1 (full cost only)
- **Path Reconstruction:** Track predecessor states to identify which edge receives the discount

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithCoupon(graph, s, t) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [Infinity, Infinity]);
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [null, null]);
  const pq = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => a.dist < b.dist);

  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.push({ u: s, used: 0, dist: 0 });

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const { u, used, dist: d } = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if (u === t) break;

    for (const { v, w } of graph[u]) {
      // Transition without using coupon
      if (d + w < dist[v][used]) {
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = { u, used };
        pq.push({ u: v, used, dist: dist[v][used] });
      }
      // Transition using coupon (only if not yet used)
      if (used === 0) {
        const newDist = d + w / 2;
        if (newDist < dist[v][1]) {
          dist[v][1] = newDist;
          prev[v][1] = { u, used, discounted: { from: u, to: v, weight: w } };
          pq.push({ u: v, used: 1, dist: newDist });
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const best = dist[t][0] < dist[t][1] ? 0 : 1;
  return { distance: dist[t][best], finalState: best };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to k coupons instead of one?
- How would you allow the discount on any one edge (not just along a shortest path)?
- How does this change if the coupon provides a fixed discount rather than percentage reduction?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests stability, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs between sorting algorithms. It asks for a concrete approach rather than a generic answer.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sorting
- Bottom-up merge sort
- Time/space complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSort(arr){
  const n = arr.length; 
  const aux = new Array(n);
  for (let width = 1; width < n; width *= 2){
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += width * 2){
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, n);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, n);
      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right){
        if (arr[p].score <= arr[q].score) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
        else aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      for (let t = left; t < right; t++) arr[t] = aux[t];
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for memory-constrained environments where n is very large?
- Compare stability guarantees and real-time performance between mergesort and quicksort in streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about DAGs, timing, and dynamic programming over graphs, a common pattern in build pipelines, data workflows, or task scheduling.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort to linearize a DAG
- DP on DAG to propagate earliest start times
- In-degree based BFS (Kahn) and cycle detection

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestTimes(n, duration, edges) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { g[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const es = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let visited = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    visited++;
    for (const v of g[u]) {
      es[v] = Math.max(es[v], es[u] + duration[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const total = Math.max(...es.map((s, i) => s + duration[i]));
  return { earliestStart: es, totalDuration: total };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to detect cycles and report the cycle path?
- How would you extend to handle parallel resources or varying durations per edge?

